%0 Proceeding
%T Proceedings of the 2025 ACM SIGSAC Conference on Computer and Communications Security
%@ 9798400715259
%X On behalf of the ACM CCS 2025 Organizing Committee, we warmly welcome you to the 32nd ACM SIGSAC Conference on Computer and Communications Security (CCS). We are delighted that, for the first time, ACM CCS is being held in Taipei, Taiwan. This year’s conference theme is “Continuity, Creativity, Community”, reflecting our shared commitment to building on the rich legacy of CCS, fostering innovation in security and privacy research, and strengthening our global community.The main conference this year features 316 research presentations, showcasing the latest innovations and developments in security and privacy. We are honored to welcome two distinguished keynote speakers—Prof. Giovanni Vigna (University of California, Santa Barbara) and Prof. David Basin (ETH Zurich)—who will share their insights on pressing challenges shaping the future of our discipline. Complementing the main program, 21 specialized workshops will be held on the pre- and post-conference days, offering venues for in-depth discussion and collaboration on focused research topics.
%I Association for Computing Machinery
%D 2025
%C Taipei, Taiwan

%0 Journal Article
%T A Causal Learning Framework for Enhancing Robustness of Source Code Models
%V 2
%U https://doi.org/10.1145/3729387
%R 10.1145/3729387
%X Deep Learning (DL) models are useful for many software engineering tasks. However, these models are susceptible to adversarial attacks, partly because they learn spurious features that incur spurious correlations between these features and model predictions. In this paper, we tackle the problem with a novel causal learning framework, dubbed CausalCode, which leverages causal inference principles to mitigate spurious correlations. At a high level, CausalCode can be characterized as follows: (i) it uses causal data augmentation to generate intervention examples to disrupt spurious correlations; (ii) it leverages regularization to learn invariant representations that prefer causal features to spurious features; (iii) it can enhance the robustness of multiple DL models for source code-based software engineering tasks because it is task-agnostic and model-agnostic. To evaluate its effectiveness, we conduct comprehensive experiments on two models (i.e., CodeBERT and GraphCodeBERT), with respect to four software engineering tasks (i.e., defect detection, functionality classification, code translation, and code repair). Experimental results show that CausalCode outperforms the state-of-the-art approaches in enhancing the robustness of these models.
%N FSE
%J Proc. ACM Softw. Eng.
%I Association for Computing Machinery
%A Junyao Ye
%A Zhen Li
%A Xi Tang
%A Deqing Zou
%A Shouhuai Xu
%A Weizhong Qiang
%A Hai Jin
%D 2025
%P Article FSE117
%K Causal Learning, Adversarial Robustness, Code Models

