%0 Journal Article
%T Bounded Verification of Multi-threaded Programs via Lazy Sequentialization
%V 44
%@ 0164-0925
%U https://doi.org/10.1145/3478536
%R 10.1145/3478536
%X Bounded verification techniques such as bounded model checking (BMC) have successfully been used for many practical program analysis problems, but concurrency still poses a challenge. Here, we describe a new approach to BMC of sequentially consistent imperative programs that use POSIX threads. We first translate the multi-threaded program into a nondeterministic sequential program that preserves reachability for all round-robin schedules with a given bound on the number of rounds. We then reuse existing high-performance BMC tools as backends for the sequential verification problem. Our translation is carefully designed to introduce very small memory overheads and very few sources of nondeterminism, so it produces tight SAT/SMT formulae, and is thus very effective in practice: Our Lazy-CSeq tool implementing this translation for the C programming language won several gold and silver medals in the concurrency category of the Software Verification Competitions (SV-COMP) 2014–2021 and was able to find errors in programs where all other techniques (including testing) failed. In this article, we give a detailed description of our translation and prove its correctness, sketch its implementation using the CSeq framework, and report on a detailed evaluation and comparison of our approach.
%N 1
%J ACM Trans. Program. Lang. Syst.
%I Association for Computing Machinery
%A Omar Inverso
%A Ermenegildo Tomasco
%A Bernd Fischer
%A Salvatore La Torre
%A Gennaro Parlato
%D 2021
%P Article 1
%K Concurrent program analysis, sequentialization

%0 Conference Paper
%T Scaver: A Scalable Verification System for Programmable Network
%@ 9798400707148
%U https://doi.org/10.1145/3672199.3673887
%R 10.1145/3672199.3673887
%X The application of symbolic execution in the field of programmable network verification suffers from a lack of scalability due to path explosion and substantial computational resources consumption required for constraint solving. In this paper, we propose Scaver, a scalable verification system based on symbolic execution for programmable network. To address the path explosion problem, we develop multiple pruning strategies to reduce the number of execution paths without affecting the validity of verification, by precisely identifying the execution paths that are related to the verification purpose. To deal with the substantial computational resources consumption problem of constraint solving, we put forward an analytical results reuse strategy, avoiding repetitively computing the same constraints. Furthermore, an assertion language named PropLang is designed, which not only fulfills the demands of assertion language design, but also guides the implementation of symbolic execution optimization strategies. Experimental results on open-source programs demonstrate that Scaver is capable of effectively detecting potential network errors and alleviating scalability issue of symbolic execution. Compared to ASSERT-P4, Scaver reduces the number of execution paths and execution time up to 53%, 46%, respectively.
%B Proceedings of the 2024 SIGCOMM Workshop on Formal Methods Aided Network Operation
%I Association for Computing Machinery
%A Ying Yao
%A Zixi Cui
%A Le Tian
%A Menglong Li
%A Fan Pan
%A Yuxiang Hu
%D 2024
%P 14–19
%K Formal Methods, P4 Verification, Programmable Network
%C Sydney, NSW, Australia

