%A Philip S. Yu
%D 2022
%P 149–153
%K toolkit, open source, deep learning, code representation, code intelligence, code embedding, benchmark
%C Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania

%0 Conference Paper
%T Towards an Appropriate Level of Reliance on AI: A Preliminary Reliance-Control Framework for AI in Software Engineering
%@ 9798400726361
%U https://doi.org/10.1145/3803437.3806704
%R 10.1145/3803437.3806704
%X How software developers interact with Artificial Intelligence (AI)-powered tools, including Large Language Models (LLMs), plays a vital role in how these AI-powered tools impact them. While overreliance on AI may lead to long-term negative consequences (e.g., atrophy of critical thinking skills); underreliance might deprive software developers of potential gains in productivity and quality. Based on twenty-two interviews with software developers on using LLMs for software development, we propose a preliminary reliance-control framework where the level of control can be used as a way to identify AI overreliance and underreliance. We also use it to recommend future research to further explore the different control levels supported by the current and emergent LLM-driven tools. Our paper contributes to the emerging discourse on AI overreliance and provides an understanding of the appropriate degree of reliance as essential to developers making the most of these powerful technologies. Our findings can help practitioners, educators, and policymakers promote responsible and effective use of AI tools.
%B Proceedings of the 34th ACM International Conference on the Foundations of Software Engineering
%I Association for Computing Machinery
%A Samuel Ferino
%A Rashina Hoda
%A John Grundy
%A Christoph Treude
%D 2026
%P 1795–1799
%K artificial intelligence, software development, developer tools, human-AI interaction, large language models
%C Concordia University, Montreal, QC, Canada

%0 Conference Paper
%T Compiler analysis for OpenMP tasks correctness
%@ 9781450333580
%U https://doi.org/10.1145/2742854.2742882
%R 10.1145/2742854.2742882
%X OpenMP has become the most frequently used programming model for shared memory parallel systems by virtue of its simplicity and scalability. Notwithstanding its easiness, the constant evolution of OpenMP brings forth difficulties when it comes to keep up with its capabilities. Compilers are key tools to anticipate bugs that may appear at runtime. Despite this, most compilers do not diagnose common mistakes that cause execution errors. Furthermore, many compilers do not yet implement the latest extensions of the OpenMP asynchronous model, and there are no checking systems to properly identify errors in that model.In this paper, we focus on the mistakes derived from the usage of OpenMP tasks. We define a data-flow algorithm that computes the live tasks at every point of the program. Based on that, we present different scenarios and the analyses that allow detecting possible runtime failures, loss of performance or non-deterministic results. We have implemented all the analyses in the Mercurium source-to-source compiler, which, based on the results, suggests changes to the user in order to avoid runtime problems. We test our implementation with over 70 students and 6 benchmarks, as well as with the results of the Oracle Solaris Studio 12.3 compiler, which warns about correctness issues related to the scope of variables in OpenMP.
%B Proceedings of the 12th ACM International Conference on Computing Frontiers
%I Association for Computing Machinery
%A Sara Royuela
%A Roger Ferrer
%A Diego Caballero
%A Xavier Martorell
%D 2015
%P Article 7
%K tasks, static analysis, correctness, compiler, OpenMP
%C Ischia, Italy

%0 Journal Article
%T Beautified QR Code for Museum Audiovisual Assistant System
%V 19
%@ 1556-4673
%U https://doi.org/10.1145/3786778
%R 10.1145/3786778
%X The preservation of museum collections worldwide constitutes a vital aspect of tangible cultural heritage, a legacy that must be transmitted to future generations to facilitate their understanding of historical experiences and ancestral encounters. Traditional QR codes, typically presented in black and white, are limited by their lack of content indication, often leading to confusion in simplifying the process for human viewers to identify various QR codes as they refer to different website links with abundant information, particularly in exhibition settings. Integrating “Environmental, Social, and Governance” factors within “Corporate Social Responsibility” frameworks has opened avenues for innovative technological applications in the cultural sector. This study explores the utility of beautified QR codes as a cost-effective and efficient alternative to traditional audio guides, offering enhanced user experiences through seamless integration with personal mobile devices, including smartphones and tablets. This article proposes implementing advanced QR code beautification techniques, specifically BlendMask and Gaussian-Jordan elimination techniques, to embed images within QR codes, thereby enhancing object detection and mitigating the aforementioned issues. The study advocates for the use of beautified QR codes as an innovative medium that integrates visual displays with the presentation of museum collections, complementing conventional multimedia guides and other audiovisual aids. Additionally, deploying colorful and aesthetically refined QR codes offers cultural institutions an efficient means of promoting their collections and brand images, potentially reducing marketing expenditures on social media and other promotional platforms. A user survey was conducted to assess the reception of beautified QR codes, examining user behavior and attitudes toward utilizing smartphones and audio guides in this context. The findings suggest that picture-embedded QR codes, when applied to museum captions, can significantly enhance the global audience’s experience, providing a more immersive and accessible approach to engaging with national and international cultural treasures via mobile devices.
%N 2
%J J. Comput. Cult. Herit.
%I Association for Computing Machinery
%A Min-Jen Tsai
%A Ya-Chu Lee
%A Hung-Yu Wu
%D 2026
%P Article 20
%K Quick Response (QR) code, Image Segmentation, National Palace Museum, Reed-Solomon (RS) Code

%0 Conference Paper
%T Kokkos Comm: Performance Portable Communication for Distributed Kokkos Applications
%@ 9798400727344
%U https://doi.org/10.1145/3815572.3815748
%R 10.1145/3815572.3815748
%X This paper introduces Kokkos Comm, a new library and API specification for enhanced performance, portability, and productivity. Kokkos is a widely used C++ performance portability ecosystem that addresses performance portability on-node through advanced C++ metaprogramming, including GPU and OpenMP targeting support. Kokkos Comm addresses challenges of integrating Kokkos with distributed memory programming models. Kokkos Comm alleviates accidental complexity associated with coordinating non-blocking communication operations and Kokkos execution spaces, as well as handling non-contiguous Kokkos: Views. Automation of common low-level, error-prone implementation details, such as packing and unpacking of non-contiguous data, increases programmer productivity while decreasing code complexity with the added potential for higher performance (e.g., vs. MPI derived datatypes).Further, this library serves as a platform for researching improved methods for managing non-contiguous data and exploring new communication APIs with performance portability across various underlying transports and accelerators. For instance, Kokkos Comm enables use of varied internal implementations of the data transfer functionality (e.g., MPI RMA, NCCL, SHMEM dialects, lib-fabric), while maintaining overall support for MPI elsewhere in an application (e.g., ScaLAPACK).
%B Proceedings of the Platform for Advanced Scientific Computing Conference
%I Association for Computing Machinery
%A C. Nicole Avans
%A Gabriel Dos Santos
%A C{\'e}dric Chevalier
