%0 Conference Paper
%T Enhancing Semantic Understanding in Pointer Analysis using Large Language Models
%@ 9798400721489
%U https://doi.org/10.1145/3759425.3763393
%R 10.1145/3759425.3763393
%X Pointer analysis has been studied for over four decades. However, existing frameworks continue to suffer from the propagation of incorrect facts. A major limitation stems from their insufficient semantic understanding of code, resulting in overly conservative treatment of user-defined functions. Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) present new opportunities to bridge this gap. In this paper, we propose LMPA (LLM-enhanced Pointer Analysis), a vision that integrates LLMs into pointer analysis to enhance both precision and scalability. LMPA identifies user-definedfunctions that resemble system APIs and models them accordingly, thereby mitigating erroneous cross-calling-context propagation. Furthermore, it enhances summary-based analysis by inferring initial points-to sets and introducing a novel summary strategy augmented with natural language.  Finally, we discuss the key challenges involved in realizing this vision.
%B Proceedings of the 1st ACM SIGPLAN International Workshop on Language Models and Programming Languages
%I Association for Computing Machinery
%A Baijun Cheng
%A Kailong Wang
%A Ling Shi
%A Haoyu Wang
%A Yao Guo
%A Ding Li
%A Xiangqun Chen
%D 2025
%P 112–117
%K LLM, pointer analysis, semantic understanding
%C Singapore, Singapore

